  "long_sentences": [
    {
      "start_s": 48.28,
      "end_s": 58.82,
      "midpoint_s": 53.55,
      "text": "By the end of this film, you'll know how a party trick, a country doctor, and a room full of doubters turned surgery from a race into something you could sleep through.",
      "cuts": [
        {
          "row": "P1:07b",
          "s": 52.89,
          "left": "doctor,",
          "right": "and"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start_s": 78.66,
      "end_s": 86.78,
      "midpoint_s": 82.72,
      "text": "Surgeons had wished for something better for centuries, and the strange thing is the answer had already been written down nearly fifty years earlier.",
      "cuts": [
        {
          "row": "P1:09c",
          "s": 81.68,
          "left": "centuries,",
          "right": "and"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start_s": 122.12,
      "end_s": 130.94,
      "midpoint_s": 126.53,
      "text": "Traveling showmen let audiences breathe them on stage, and young people held ether frolics where guests staggered around laughing.",
      "cuts": [
        {
          "row": "P2:05b",
          "s": 125.76,
          "left": "stage,",
          "right": "and"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start_s": 223.5,
      "end_s": 231.6,
      "midpoint_s": 227.55,
      "text": "On the sixteenth of October, 1846, Morton walked into the same Boston hospital carrying a glass globe.",
      "cuts": [
        {
          "row": "P3:06a",
          "s": 226.68,
          "left": "1846,",
          "right": "Morton"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start_s": 281.36,
      "end_s": 289.64,
      "midpoint_s": 285.5,
      "text": "He called it Letheon after a river in the old Greek stories that made people forget, and he took out a patent on it.",
      "cuts": [
        {
          "row": "P4:03a_arch_letheon_patent",
          "s": 285.86,
          "left": "stories",
          "right": "that"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start_s": 305.9,
      "end_s": 314.16,
      "midpoint_s": 310.03,
      "text": "He suggested a new word for this sleep without pain, anesthesia, from the Greek for without feeling.",
      "cuts": [
        {
          "row": "P4:04c_cg_anesthesia_etymology",
          "s": 309.2,
          "left": "pain,",
          "right": "anesthesia,"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start_s": 409.94,
      "end_s": 419.56,
      "midpoint_s": 414.75,
      "text": "One evening, he and two friends sniffed a liquid called chloroform at his dinner table, and moments later, all three were out cold on the floor.",
      "cuts": [
        {
          "row": "P5:05b",
          "s": 415.48,
          "left": "table,",
          "right": "and"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start_s": 427.08,
      "end_s": 437.52,
      "midpoint_s": 432.3,
      "text": "Then in 1853, a London doctor called John Snow gave chloroform to Queen Victoria for the birth of her son, Prince Leopold.",
      "cuts": [
        {
          "row": "P5:07a_arch_snow_chloroform_1858",
          "s": 429.63,
          "left": "1853,",
          "right": "a"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start_s": 452.72,
      "end_s": 461.22,
      "midpoint_s": 456.97,
      "text": "Today, for a healthy adult, deaths caused by an anesthetic are very rare, about one in every hundred thousand.",
